# Generated test harness from the self-host `test` verb (ADR 7.8.26a §2.3).
# `compile_and_run_tests` writes a harness source and its binary beside the file
# under test and removes neither — deleting them needs a `tg_remove_file` FFI
# that does not exist yet (ADR 7.8.26b). The leading underscore is what keeps
# them out of `make tg-test`'s `src/compiler/test_*.tg` glob; ignoring them here
# keeps them out of `git status`, which is the other way a leftover goes
# unnoticed. One pattern covers both artifacts: the source is
# `_tgtest_<name>.tg` and the binary is the same path without the extension.
_tgtest_*

# Scripts
scripts/*.py

# Anything that isn't a .tg file in examples/
examples/**/*
!examples/README.md
!examples/**/*.tg
!examples/**/

# Static linking
*.a

# Claude
!.claude/settings.local.json

# Temp
.tmp

# Reports
forbidden-actions-report.md
ignore-access-report.md

# Mutation-sweep litter at the repo root (ADR 17.7.26g close-out).
# A sweep RUNS each mutant, including ones that corrupt a test's temp-dir
# helper: `fn tmpdir() -> PathBuf` mutated to `Default::default()` returns an
# EMPTY path, so every `dir.join(…)` in that test resolves against the process
# CWD — the repo root under `cargo test`. The test then fails (the mutant is
# correctly Caught), but only AFTER writing its fixtures here, so no assertion
# inside the helper can prevent the files: the mutant skips the helper body
# entirely and the CALLER does the writing.
#
# These are therefore unavoidable side effects of a healthy sweep, not a
# defect to fix in the tests. Ignoring them by name stops the real harm —
# commit f925b4b5 swept three of them into a commit, where two SHADOWED the
# real 356-line and 24-line allowlists in tools/. Removing the cause would
# mean either making the helpers return a type with no `Default` (so the
# mutant is unviable and never runs) or running sweeps with CWD outside the
# repo; both are crate-wide test-hygiene work, tracked separately.
/allowlist.toml
/baseline.toml
/dateless.toml
/mutation-baseline.toml
/mutant-survivor-allowlist.toml
/mutant-timeout-allowlist.toml
/outcomes.json
/caught.txt
/missed.txt
/missed_ids.txt
/missed_allowlisted.txt
/timeout.txt
/unviable.txt
/trivial.rs

# Golden compile-artifact leak (ADR 31.7.26b D1).
#
# `tungsten compile tests/golden/run/foo.tg` emits the executable at
# `tests/golden/run/foo` — an extensionless sibling of its own fixture. The
# retired shell runner's `rm -f` cleanup ran only after a successful compare, so
# a compile that produced a binary and then failed left it behind; five
# devcontainer-built ELF aarch64 executables reached git that way.
#
# Expressed as "ignore anything with no extension" rather than an allowlist of
# fixture suffixes: nothing tracked under tests/golden/ is extensionless, and an
# allowlist would silently swallow a NEW fixture kind — the "green because it
# never looked" shape this ADR exists to remove.
tests/golden/**
!tests/golden/**/
!tests/golden/**/*.*
# Re-ignore, because the directory negation above out-ranks the general rules
# earlier in this file: without it, `.tungsten/` (line 14) stops being ignored
# under tests/golden/ and every golden run leaves untracked elaboration cache.
#
# The trailing slash is load-bearing (it scopes the rule to directories), and it
# makes `git check-ignore` misleading as a probe: on a path that does not exist
# on disk git cannot tell it is a directory, so `git check-ignore -q
# tests/golden/test/.tungsten` reports NOT-ignored even though the rule is
# correct. Probe it with the directory actually present, or check a file inside
# it — `git status` is the honest test.
tests/golden/**/.tungsten/

# Lane bookkeeping (ADR 4.8.26c D2). A lane worktree carries its own manifest at
# `.lane/manifest.toml`, written by the `lane` tool rather than committed — it
# lives and dies with the worktree, which is what makes it crash-consistent.
# Ignored here so a lane cut from this branch reads clean under a hand-run
# `git status`; `lane`'s own dirtiness check excludes it via pathspec regardless,
# so a lane cut from an older branch still behaves.
.lane/
